WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.269 --> 00:00:02.360
It is -38° in here.

00:00:02.360 --> 00:00:05.120
I have never been anywhere this cold.

00:00:05.120 --> 00:00:08.189
I've had to insulate my camera so the batteries
keep working,

00:00:08.189 --> 00:00:12.440
and keep my microphone close to my body so
the wiring doesn't freeze and fall apart.

00:00:12.440 --> 00:00:14.959
This is the United States
National Ice Core Laboratory,

00:00:14.959 --> 00:00:17.100
in Denver, Colorado,

00:00:17.100 --> 00:00:20.200
and I figured it was best to interview
the scientists that work here

00:00:20.200 --> 00:00:22.470
outside this giant freezer.

00:00:22.470 --> 00:00:25.430
We have about 20,000 metres of ice
represented here

00:00:25.430 --> 00:00:29.980
and that's from probably, anywhere from 60-80
different boreholes around the world.

00:00:29.980 --> 00:00:33.530
The deepest one we have is about 3,600 metres
through the ice sheet of Antarctica.

00:00:33.530 --> 00:00:37.500
In our lab, the oldest ice that we have is
about 420,000 years old

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:41.630
and that's from the Vostok Ice Core, drilled
in east Antarctica, back in the mid-90s.

00:00:41.630 --> 00:00:44.230
When a new core comes in,

00:00:44.230 --> 00:00:48.070
we piece it up and ship it out
to the principal investigators.

00:00:48.070 --> 00:00:50.710
All of this is meteoric ice,

00:00:50.710 --> 00:00:53.950
which means ice that's fallen from the sky.

00:00:53.950 --> 00:00:57.989
They don't want any of the tundra or sea ice,

00:00:57.989 --> 00:01:01.050
because it would contaminate
the meteoric ice that we have.

00:01:01.050 --> 00:01:03.700
One of the principal investigators who was
running an analysis

00:01:03.700 --> 00:01:05.920
can check his system from home,

00:01:05.920 --> 00:01:09.549
and he asked if somebody was eating
potato chips in the break room

00:01:09.549 --> 00:01:14.960
because he started seeing a
higher amount of sodium in his samples.

00:01:16.280 --> 00:01:18.740
And they, said, well... yeah(!)

00:01:18.740 --> 00:01:20.119
That's how sensitive this is.

00:01:20.119 --> 00:01:24.320
If the mains fail, we have
a natural-gas-fuelled generator out in back,

00:01:24.320 --> 00:01:27.469
it automatically switches power
to the freezer building.

00:01:27.469 --> 00:01:30.709
We're 100% backed up. On everything.

00:01:30.709 --> 00:01:35.240
Each one of these tubes contains
a sample of ice from the Arctic or Antarctic.

00:01:35.240 --> 00:01:38.499
And the deeper underground the core
was taken from, the older it is:

00:01:38.499 --> 00:01:43.749
all that fallen snow crushed down over perhaps
hundreds of thousands of years,

00:01:43.749 --> 00:01:46.999
along with anything else that happened to
be dragged along from the atmosphere.

00:01:46.999 --> 00:01:48.279
Pretty much anything that's in the atmosphere

00:01:48.279 --> 00:01:51.889
whether it's the atmospheric gases, or dust,
or anything like that,

00:01:51.889 --> 00:01:54.310
eventually lands on the ice sheets
of Antarctica and Greenland

00:01:54.310 --> 00:01:56.880
and will get buried and trapped
within the snow.

00:01:56.880 --> 00:01:59.460
And as that compresses into ice,
it preserves that information

00:01:59.460 --> 00:02:01.770
so we can actually use these ice cores

00:02:01.770 --> 00:02:04.310
to recreate what our
atmospheric chemistry was like

00:02:04.310 --> 00:02:06.840
dating back to about 850,000 years ago.

00:02:06.840 --> 00:02:10.130
We can also determine what global temperatures
were and how they changed over time

00:02:10.130 --> 00:02:13.520
by studying the stable isotopes of oxygen
within the water molecules.

00:02:13.520 --> 00:02:17.170
Essentially, they're looking at the ratio
between ¹⁶O and ¹⁸O.

00:02:17.170 --> 00:02:19.000
And by using a lot of modern-day studies,

00:02:19.000 --> 00:02:20.560
they can figure out what the temperature was

00:02:20.560 --> 00:02:22.450
in the area where that snow fell.

00:02:22.450 --> 00:02:25.110
There's a wide varieties of ways that we can
date these ice cores

00:02:25.110 --> 00:02:28.740
with Greenland cores, often times we can actual
see visible layers

00:02:28.740 --> 00:02:32.010
that change from summer to winter, based on
the dust concentration

00:02:32.010 --> 00:02:34.920
so, sort of like tree rings, count those seasons
going backwards

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:36.460
and get a date that way.

00:02:36.460 --> 00:02:39.110
And then occasionally we'll run across things
like volcanic ash layers

00:02:39.110 --> 00:02:41.070
and if there's enough material
within an ash layer

00:02:41.070 --> 00:02:44.750
we can use radiometric dating to get
a very specific age on that volcanic layer.

00:02:44.750 --> 00:02:46.990
The margin of error in terms of our ages

00:02:46.990 --> 00:02:49.720
are usually within, you know,
±10 years or so.

00:02:49.720 --> 00:02:51.660
So it's pretty accurate, especially when we're
looking at ice

00:02:51.660 --> 00:02:53.670
that's a few hundred thousand years old.

00:02:53.670 --> 00:02:56.590
By understanding the natural variations of
climate change

00:02:56.590 --> 00:02:58.820
we can better predict what's going to happen
moving forward

00:02:58.820 --> 00:03:01.690
now that humanity has made
a pretty significant impact

00:03:01.690 --> 00:03:03.130
on what's going on with our planet

00:03:03.130 --> 00:03:06.050
in terms of the amount of greenhouse gases
that we've put into the atmosphere

00:03:06.050 --> 00:03:07.050
over the last century.

00:03:07.050 --> 00:03:08.390
We can better understand the past

00:03:08.390 --> 00:03:09.740
and using that knowledge of the past

00:03:09.740 --> 00:03:12.600
we can better model what's going to happen
moving forward into the future.

00:03:12.600 --> 00:03:13.790
Thank you to all the team here

00:03:13.790 --> 00:03:16.020
at the US National Ice Core Laboratory.

00:03:16.020 --> 00:03:18.570
Pull down the description for more about them
and about their work

00:03:18.570 --> 00:03:20.550
and I think my eyelids are sticking together?

00:03:20.550 --> 00:03:23.510
(You can't see it, but you do have frost all
on your eyelashes.)

00:03:23.510 --> 00:03:24.460
Really? (Yeah!)

